\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two directions along one circle}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Under a [period-preserving signed permutation](fdip-0164), the
character value at step #{e_{\pi(j)}} is its former value at
#{b^{-1}e_{\pi(j)}=s_j e_j}. A negative sign inverts the multiplier.
For a single circle of period #{N}, reversal therefore takes the
residue #{v} to #{-v}. Its orbit contains two labels when #{2v\ne0}, and
one label when #{2v=0}. The latter holds at zero and also at #{N/2}
for even #{N}. These are all the solutions: a representative
#{0\le v<N} has #{0\le 2v<2N}, so a multiple of #{N} in that range
is either zero or #{N}.}

\p{For #{N=1} or #{N=2}, every site itself equals its negative.
Reversing the labeled direction then acts identically on sites,
although its sign differs from that of the identity in #{B_\Lambda}.
For #{N\ge3}, the forward and backward steps from zero are distinct
neighbors.}
